---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadAreas, distKm, median, fmt } from '../../data';
import { OVO_AFFILIATE_URL } from '../../config';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const all = loadAreas().filter(a => a.kwh !== null);
  const sorted = [...all].sort((a, b) => b.kwh! - a.kwh!);
  const med = median(all.map(a => a.kwh!));
  return all.map(area => {
    const rank = sorted.findIndex(a => a.slug === area.slug) + 1;
    const nearby = all.filter(a => a.slug !== area.slug)
      .map(a => ({ a, d: distKm(area, a) })).sort((x, y) => x.d - y.d).slice(0, 5);
    return { params: { slug: area.slug }, props: { area, rank, total: all.length, med, best: sorted[0], worst: sorted[sorted.length - 1], nearby } };
  });
}
const { area, rank, total, med, best, worst, nearby } = Astro.props;
const kwh = area.kwh!;
const diffPct = Math.round(((kwh - med) / med) * 100);
const system = 4;
const annual = kwh * system;
const pctile = Math.round(((total - rank) / (total - 1)) * 100);
const band = pctile >= 75 ? 'among the sunnier' : pctile >= 40 ? 'around the middle of' : pctile >= 15 ? 'in the lower half of' : 'among the least sunny';
const vs = diffPct === 0 ? 'in line with the UK median' : diffPct > 0 ? `${diffPct}% above the UK median` : `${Math.abs(diffPct)}% below the UK median`;
const spread = Math.round(((best.kwh! - worst.kwh!) / worst.kwh!) * 100);
---
<Base title={`Are solar panels worth it in ${area.name}? Local output data | WhatsWattWorth`} description={`A 1 kW solar system in ${area.name} is estimated to generate ${fmt(kwh)} kWh a year, ${vs}. See how ${area.name} ranks among ${total} UK local authorities.`} canonical={`/solar/${area.slug}`}>
<main class="page"><div class="wrap">
<div class="eyebrow">Solar data for {area.name}</div>
<h1>Are solar panels worth it in {area.name}?</h1>
<p class="lede">A standard south-facing solar system in {area.name} is estimated to generate about <strong>{fmt(kwh)} kWh a year for every 1 kW of panels</strong>. That is {vs}, and ranks {area.name} {rank} out of {total} UK local authorities.</p>

<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><span class="num">Output</span><h3>{fmt(kwh)} kWh per kW</h3><p>Estimated yearly generation for 1 kW of panels, south-facing at a 35° tilt.</p></div>
<div class="card"><span class="num">Rank</span><h3>{rank} of {total}</h3><p>Where {area.name} sits when all UK local authorities are ordered from highest to lowest output.</p></div>
<div class="card"><span class="num">Typical home</span><h3>about {fmt(Math.round(annual))} kWh</h3><p>For a {system} kW system, a common size on a UK home. Yours will depend on your roof.</p></div>
<div class="card"><span class="num">Vs median</span><h3>{diffPct > 0 ? '+' : ''}{diffPct}%</h3><p>Compared with the UK median of {fmt(Math.round(med))} kWh per kW per year.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What this means for {area.name}</h2>
<p>{area.name} is {band} UK local authorities for solar output. Across the country the gap between the best and worst areas is about {spread}%: {best.name} comes out highest at {fmt(best.kwh!)} kWh per kW, and {worst.name} lowest at {fmt(worst.kwh!)}. A higher figure means each panel produces more electricity over the year, which usually shortens the time it takes for a system to pay for itself, all else being equal.</p>
<p>Output is only one side of the sum. What you save depends on the system's cost, how much of the electricity you use yourself rather than export, and the tariffs you are on. We do not publish savings figures because those depend on your own circumstances, so ask installers to show their assumptions and compare at least three quotes.</p>

<h2>Nearby areas for comparison</h2>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse">
<thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">Area</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">kWh per kW per year</th><th style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">Distance</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><strong>{area.name}</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><strong>{fmt(kwh)}</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">-</td></tr>
{nearby.map(n => <tr><td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><a href={`/solar/${n.a.slug}`}>{n.a.name}</a></td><td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">{fmt(n.a.kwh!)}</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)">{Math.round(n.d)} km</td></tr>)}
</tbody></table>

{OVO_AFFILIATE_URL && (
<div class="notice"><p><strong>Want a quote?</strong> You can request a solar quote from OVO through this link. We may earn a commission if you do, at no extra cost to you. <a href={OVO_AFFILIATE_URL} rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">Request a quote</a>. See our <a href="/disclosure">affiliate disclosure</a>.</p></div>
)}

<h2>How we calculated this</h2>
<p>The figure comes from the European Commission's PVGIS tool, using a point near the centre of {area.name}, a 1 kW system, a 35° tilt facing south and 14% system losses. Real roofs differ: shading, a different angle or an east-west layout will change the result. Full details are on our <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a>.</p>
<p class="updated">Data source: PVGIS v5.2, European Commission Joint Research Centre. Estimates only, not a quote or financial advice.</p>
</div></main>
</Base>
